[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked **known: one issue, one fix queued**. Read report by report, it
holds three problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one bug. Fixing the parser leaves the booking-v3 form hint and the one-day move open.
Each now has a ticket of its own.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

## How the reports were sorted

1. **One report, one count.** The export repeats some reports across its pages. Each id is counted once.
2. **Read the body, not the subject.** Most bodies name two dates: what was booked and what arrived.
   In almost all of them the second is the first with day and month swapped. A swap is a flip bug.
3. **Flip bugs split by tenant and channel, not by intake tag.** Every tenant on file writes the day first.
   A report through the form of a `booking-v3` tenant is `hint`: that form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under
   its date field. Everything else is `parser`. The intake tag is a guess from the browser and is often wrong.
4. **A one-day move is not a swap.** The reports the desk already marked as a one-day move
   ([desk notes](https://github.com/JamesNock/support-kb-4471/blob/main/src/lib/data/desk-notes.json))
   describe a date that moves by a single day: the weekday is off by one, a client in Lisbon and a client in
   Toronto see different days, an invite arrives before the booking was made. A month-first parser cannot do
   that. The desk files them under `parser` by tenant and footnotes them; they stay counted there, and
   here they are also a problem of their own.

## The old view, for comparison

The table on the incident page, unchanged. Same counts for `parser` and `hint`; the one-day moves are only a footnote there.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line alone. All three problems
come in as "wrong day" and "wrong date", so all three landed in one ticket. Subjects tell you the
symptom; the tenant, the channel and the body tell you the bug.

